A.eTo=s(["Monitor renal, hepatic, electrolyte, and hematologic status closely. Hypercalciuria, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, RTA, renal failure, acute hepatic failure, hypotension, and phlebitis may occur. For dosing information in renal failure, see Chapter 32.","Common infusion-related reactions include fever, chills, headache, hypotension, nausea, and vomiting; may premedicate with acetaminophen and diphenhydramine 30 min before and 4 hr after infusion. Meperidine is useful for chills. Hydrocortisone, 1 mg/mg ampho (max.: 25 mg), added to bottle may help to prevent immediate adverse reactions. Use total body weight for obese patients when calculating dosages.","Salt loading with 10\u201315 mL/kg of NS infused prior to each dose may minimize the risk of nephrotoxicity. Maintaining sodium intake of >4 mEq/kg/24 hr in premature neonates may also reduce risk for nephrotoxicity. Nephrotoxic drugs such as aminoglycosides, chemotherapeutic agents, and cyclosporine may result in synergistic toxicity. Hypokalemia may increase the toxicity of neuromuscular blocking agents and cardiac glycosides.","Although there are no breastfeeding data for amphotericin, many experts believe it is compatible since the drug is highly protein bound, has a large molecular weight, and is not absorbed orally."],t.s)
